0061,54.3264,53.8626,53.9654,55.4111,59.1052,65.5765,74.7359,85.5762,96.5655,106.02,112.871,116.938,118.664,118.821,118.015,116.776,115.174,112.965,110.015,106.412,102.326,98.0288,93.9161,90.4178,87.8233,86.1554,85.2165,84.6983,84.2722,83.692,82.8596,81.7288,80.2941,78.6364,76.8603,75.1601,73.6822,72.4206,71.3028,70.1637,68.9269,67.6278,66.3738,65.1808,64.0643,62.8928,61.679,60.5095,59.4177,58.5026],"pi_source":"reconstructed from Q (π₀ from the input.yaml p0)","q_source":"computed from the velocity over the outlet facets","summary":"P 54/119 mmHg   pulse 65   Q̄ 0.99 mL/s","exact":false,"params":{"R_p":11062.2453,"R_d":99560.2075,"C":6.791103e-07,"p0":69007.4}}],"has_windkessel":true,"stabilization":[{"group":"Fluid","name":"Streamline diffusion (SUPG/SD)","value":"off","detail":"Adds artificial diffusion along the streamlines to damp the spurious oscillations Galerkin advection produces at high cell Péclet number. 'length_scale: metric' sizes it from the element metric rather than a scalar diameter, which matters on the stretched cells an ALE mesh develops.","active":false},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Backflow stabilization","value":"boundaries [2, 3, 4, 5, 6]","detail":"Penalises inflow through an outlet. Without it, reverse flow over part of an outlet cap during diastole feeds unbounded kinetic energy back into the domain and the solve diverges — the classic open-outlet instability.","active":true},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Forced-normal flow","value":"off","detail":"Constrains the velocity at a boundary to stay parallel to the face normal, suppressing in-plane drift at inlets and outlets.","active":false},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Pressure fixing","value":"off","detail":"Pins the pressure at one point to remove the constant null space. Not needed when an outlet prescribes pressure — a Windkessel does, which is why it is off here.","active":false},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Velocity–pressure coupling","value":"fractionalstep  ·  scheme=CT  coupled_velocity=off  robin_bc_velocity_scheme=implicit","detail":"A fractional-step (projection) scheme splits the saddle-point system into a tentative-velocity solve, a pressure Poisson solve and a correction — cheap, at the cost of a splitting error at the boundary.","active":true},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Windkessel treatment","value":"implicit=on  condensed=on","detail":"Implicit coupling folds the 0-D outlet into the pressure operator as a low-rank correction instead of lagging it as an explicit Dirichlet value, which is what makes a compliant outlet stable at physiological R and C. 'condensed' holds the pressure constant over each outlet by condensing its DOFs.","active":true},{"group":"Fluid","name":"Windkessel tangent penalty","value":"off","detail":"Penalises tangential velocity on the Windkessel outlets.","active":false},{"group":"Mesh (ALE)","name":"Lifting operator","value":"elastic_element  ·  mu=1","detail":"Extends the interface displacement into the fluid mesh. An elastic-element operator stiffens small cells so the boundary layer is carried along rigidly and elements near the wall do not invert under large wall motion.","active":true},{"group":"Solid","name":"Incompressibility","value":"penalty quadratic  κ=1e+07","detail":"A penalty enforces near-incompressibility without a pressure unknown; κ sets how hard. Too large locks a low-order element, too small lets the wall change volume — the trade-off the penalty_parameter comment in the yaml is about.","active":true},{"group":"Solid","name":"Augmented Lagrange","value":"off","detail":"Iteratively corrects the penalty so the volume constraint is met without driving κ up.","active":false},{"group":"Solid","name":"Time integration","value":"generalized_alpha  ·  alpha_f=0.5  alpha_m=0.5  beta=0.25  gamma=0.5  stress_scheme=midpoint","detail":"Generalised-α evaluates the residual at intermediate points so high-frequency modes are damped while second-order accuracy is kept; α_m and α_f set how much. β and γ are the Newmark parameters. NOTE the mapping from α to the high-frequency dissipation ρ∞ differs between conventions, so read these against the solver's own definition before concluding how dissipative the scheme is.","active":true},{"group":"Solid","name":"Newton line search","value":"bt","detail":"Backtracking ('bt') shortens a Newton step that would increase the residual — what keeps a clamped, prestressed wall from inverting elements on the first iteration.","active":true},{"group":"Solid","name":"Quadrature degree","value":"6","detail":"Integration order for the constitutive terms. Under-integration is itself a (risky) stabilisation; over-integration costs time.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"FSI scheme","value":"newton-snes","detail":"How the fluid and solid subproblems are advanced together. A monolithic/Newton scheme avoids the added-mass instability that breaks loosely coupled schemes when the fluid and wall densities are comparable — as they are for blood and tissue.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"Interface relaxation","value":"iqn-ils  ·  max_history=30  rcond=1e-06  reuse_steps=2  step_cap=3  omega_init=0.1","detail":"IQN-ILS builds a quasi-Newton approximation from the history of interface residuals, which is what makes the fixed-point iteration converge at all under strong added mass. max_history and reuse_steps set how much history is kept.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"Traction relaxation","value":"0.5","detail":"Under-relaxes the traction handed to the solid; damps the first coupling iterations.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"Lumped traction transfer","value":"on","detail":"Transfers the interface traction through a lumped (diagonal) mass matrix, avoiding the checkerboarding a consistent transfer can produce.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"Coupling tolerance","value":"0.0001","detail":"Residual at which the interface iteration is declared converged.","active":true},{"group":"Coupling","name":"Max coupling iter